          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The notion of a quasibialgebra generalizes that of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra"} bialgebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
            by introducing a nontrivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associativity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence"} coherence
            (
            %a{:href => "#Drinfeld89"}> Drinfeld 89
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            (representable by multiplication with an element in triple tensor product) into axioms; a
            %em quasi-Hopf algebra
            is a quasi-bialgebra with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antipode"} antipode
            satisfying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            which also involve nontrivial left and right unit coherences.
          %p
            In particular, quasi-Hopf algebras may be obtained from ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebras"} Hopf algebras
            via twisting by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Drinfeld+associator"} Drinfeld associator
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra+cocycle"} bialgebra 3-cocycle
          %h3#motivation_from_quantum_field_theory Motivation from quantum field theory
          %p
            Drinfel’d was motivated by study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+CFT"} rational
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+conformal+field+theory"} 2d conformal field theory
            (RCFT) as well as by an idea from
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Esquisse+d%27un+programme"} Esquisse
            namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck-Teichm%C3%BCller+tower"} Grothendieck-Teichmüller tower
            and its modular properties. In RCFT, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            appearing can be, by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka reconstruction
            considered as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
            of
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
          %p
            A special case of the motivation in RCFT has a toy example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            which can be quite geometrically explained. Namely, where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+convolution+algebra"} groupoid convolution algebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            naturally has the structure of a Hopf algebra, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+groupoid+convolution+algebra"} twisted groupoid convolution algebra
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            equipped with a 3-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 3
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \colon \mathbf{B}G \to \mathbf{B}^3 U(1)
            is naturally a quasi-Hopf algebra. Since such a 3-cocycle is precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            of the 3d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TFT"} TFT
            called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DijkgraafPasquierRoche"} Dijkgraaf-Pasquier-Roche 91
          %h2#definition_drinfeld Definition (Drinfeld)
          %p
            A
            %strong quasibialgebra
            is a unital
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,m,\eta)
            with a structure of not necessarily
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coassociative+coalgebra"} coassociative coalgebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,\Delta,\epsilon)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            and counit
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in A\otimes A\otimes A
            such that
          %p
            (i) the coassociativity is modified by conjugation by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            in the sense
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\Delta \otimes 1)\Delta(a) = \phi\left((1\otimes\Delta)\Delta(a)\right)\phi^{-1},\,\,\,\,\,\forall a\in A,
          %p (ii) the following pentagon identity holds
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (1\otimes 1\otimes\Delta)(\phi)(\Delta\otimes 1\otimes 1)(\phi) =
                  (1\otimes\phi)(1\otimes\Delta\otimes 1)(\phi)(\phi\otimes 1)
          %p
            (iii) some identities involving unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            and counit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            hold:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo ;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\epsilon\otimes A)\Delta(a) = a = (A\otimes\epsilon)\Delta(a), \,\,\,\,\,\,a\in A;
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (A\otimes\epsilon\otimes A)\phi = 1.
          %p
            It follows that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\epsilon\otimes A\otimes A)\phi
                    \= 1 = (A\otimes A\otimes\epsilon)\phi
          %p
            The category of left
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            on the tensor product of modules
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ν
                      %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,\nu^M)
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ν
                      %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (N,\nu^N)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi M
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ν
                        %mi M
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi ν
                        %mi N
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \otimes (M\otimes N) \stackrel{\Delta\otimes M\otimes N}\longrightarrow (A\otimes A)\otimes(M\otimes N)
                  \\rightarrow (A\otimes M)\otimes (A\otimes N)\stackrel{\nu_M\otimes\nu_N}\longrightarrow M\otimes N
          %p
            Using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sweedler+notation"} Sweedler-like notation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi = \sum \phi^1\otimes \phi^2\otimes \phi^3
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi P
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≅
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Phi_{M,N,P}: (M\otimes N)\otimes P\stackrel\cong\longrightarrow M\otimes (N\otimes P)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi p
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (m\otimes n)\otimes p\mapsto \sum (\phi^1\triangleright m)
                  \\otimes ((\phi^2\triangleright n)\otimes (\phi^3\triangleright p))
          %p
            define a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            and the pentagon for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            yields the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pentagon+identity"} MacLane's pentagon
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            understood as a new
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi P
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi Q
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi N
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (M\otimes\Phi_{N,P,Q})\Phi_{M,N\otimes P,Q}(\Phi_{M,N,P}\otimes Q)=\Phi_{M,N,P\otimes Q}\Phi_{M\otimes N,P,Q}
          %p
            For this reason,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is sometimes called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
            of the quasibialgebra. While it is due to Drinfeld, another variant of it, written as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            and used in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+theory"} knot theory
            is often called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Drinfeld+associator"} Drinfeld associator
            (see there).
          %p
            A
            %strong quasi-Hopf algebra
            is a quasibialgebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ε
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, \Delta, \varepsilon, \phi)
            equipped with elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha,\beta \in A
            and an antiautomorhphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            (a suitable kind of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antipode"} antipode
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mi ε
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi i
                  %mi β
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ε
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_i S(b_i)\alpha c_i = \varepsilon (a) \alpha, \sum_i b_i\beta S(c_i) = \varepsilon(a)\beta
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(a) = \sum_i b_i \otimes c_i
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sweedler+notation"} Sweedler notation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mi β
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi where
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_i X_i\beta S(Y_i)\alpha Z_i = 1, \quad where \sum_i X_i \otimes Y_i\otimes Z_i = \phi,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi j
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi j
                  %mi β
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi where
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi j
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi j
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_j S(P_j)\alpha Q_j\beta S(R_j) =1, \quad where \sum_j P_j \otimes Q_j \otimes R_j = \phi^{-1}.
          %h3#twisting_quasibialgebras_by_2cochains Twisting quasibialgebras by 2-cochains
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is a counital 3-cocycle in the sense of bialgebra cohomology theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shahn+Majid"} Majid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pentagon+identity"} pentagon
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            = succeed "-cochains." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Thus, for a bialgebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\partial^i  = id_{A^{\otimes (i-1)}}\otimes \Delta \otimes \id_{A^{\otimes (n-i)}} : A^{\otimes n}\to A^{\otimes (n+1)},
          %p
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\leq i\leq n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo ∂
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mrow
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial^0 = 1\otimes id_{A^{\otimes n}}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mrow
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi n
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\partial^{n+1} =
                    id_{A^{\otimes n}}\otimes 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\in A^{\otimes n}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo +
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi even
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo −
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi odd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\partial^+ F = \prod_{i\,\,\,\,even} (\partial^i F),\,\,\,\,\,\partial^- F = \prod_{i\,\,\,\,odd} (\partial^i F),
          %p
            where the products are in the order of ascending
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\in A^{\otimes n}
            is a cochain then its coboundary is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo +
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta F = (\partial^+ F)(\partial^- F^{-1})
            = succeed "-cochain." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \in A^{\otimes n}
            is an
            = succeed "-cochain" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\in A^{\otimes (n+1)}
            is an
            = succeed "-cochain" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^F
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            by the formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo +
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mo ∂
                    %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi^F = (\partial^+ F)\phi(\partial^- F^{-1}).
          %p
            Drinfeld proved that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
            the following is true. Given a quasiabialgebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = (A,m,\eta,\Delta,\epsilon,\phi)
            and a 2-cochain
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^F = (A,m,\eta,F\Delta(-)F^{-1},\epsilon,\phi^F)
            is also a quasibialgebra. Furthermore,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo −
                  %mi mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A-mod
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi F
                  %mo −
                  %mi mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^F-mod
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            reflecting the idea that cohomologous cocycles lead to nonessential categorical effects. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,R)
            is quasitriangular quasibialgebra then we can twist the R-element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi H
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\in H\otimes H
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 21
                  %mi R
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^F = F_{21} R F
            to obtain quasitriangular quasibialgebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^F,R^F)
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+categories"} braided monoidal categories
            of representations are braided monoidally equivalent.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#quasifiber_functor Quasi-fiber functor
          %p
            Recall from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            that given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} rigid monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+functor"} fiber functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi RMod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C\to RMod
            one can reconstruct a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            via
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo =
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H=End(F)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi Rep
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\cong Rep(H)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Rep
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rep(H_q )
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_q
            a
            = succeed "-Hopf" do
              %em quasi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_q
            is furthermore a Hopf algebra, they do admit a weaker notion called a
            = succeed "-fiber" do
              %em quasi
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_q
          %p
            Much as a fiber functor, a quasi-fiber functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_q
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
            equipped with a natural transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mu: F(-)\otimes F(-)\to F(-\otimes -)
          %p
            The main difference is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is
            %em not
            required to satisfy the associativity condition, so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F,\mu)
            does not describe a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            to satisfy the associative condition is a direct reflection of the non-coassociativity of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_q
            %a{:href => "#EGNO16"} EGNO 2016
            for more.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hopfish+algebra"} hopfish algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitriangulated+quasi-Hopf+algebra"} quasitriangulated quasi-Hopf algebra
          %h2#references References
          %p The notion was introduced in
          %ul
            %li#Drinfeld89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Drinfel%27d"}> Vladimir Drinfel'd
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Квазихопфовы алгебры
              %strong 1
              (1989), no. 6, 114–148,
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mathnet.ru/php/getFT.phtml?jrnid=aa&paperid=53&what=fullt&option_lang=rus"} pdf
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quasi-Hopf algebras
              %strong 1
              (1990), no. 6, 1419–1457
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1047964"} MR1047964
          %p
            The relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            appeared in
          %ul#DijkgraafPasquierRoche
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robbert+Dijkgraaf"}> Robbert Dijkgraaf
              , V. Pasquier, P. Roche,
              = succeed "," do
                %em QuasiHopf algebras, group cohomology and orbifold models
              %strong 18B
              (1990), 60-72;
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quasi-quantum groups related to orbifold models
          %p and some arguments about the general relevance of quasi-Hopf algebras is in
          %ul
            %li
              Gerhard Mack,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Volker+Schomerus"} Volker Schomerus
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quasi Hopf quantum symmetry in quantum theory
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(92)90350-K"} doi
          %p Recently a monograph appeared
          %ul
            %li
              Daniel Bulacu, Stefaan Caenepeel, Florin Panaite, Freddy Van Oystaeyen,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quasi-Hopf algebras: a categorical approach
          %p
            Wikipedia article:
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quasi-Hopf_algebra"} Quasi-Hopf algebra
          %p Other articles include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                В. Г. Дринфельд,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em О структуре квазитреугольных квазихопфовых алгебр
                = succeed ":1" do
                  %strong 26
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mathnet.ru/php/getFT.phtml?jrnid=faa&paperid=768&what=fullt&option_lang=rus"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Structure of quasitriangular quasi-hopf algebras
            %li
              %p
                V. G. Drinfelʹd,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    О квазитреугольных квазихопфовых алгебрах и одной группе, тесно связанной с
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gal
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mover
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi Q
                            %mo ¯
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Q
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbf{Q}}/\mathbf {Q})
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mathnet.ru/php/getFT.phtml?jrnid=aa&paperid=199&volume=2&year=1990&issue=4&fpage=149&what=fullt&option_lang=eng"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    On quasitriangular quasi-Hopf algebras and on a group that is closely connected with
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gal
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mover
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi Q
                            %mo ¯
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Q
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbf{Q}}/\mathbf {Q})
                %strong 2
                (1991), no. 4, 829–860,
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1080203"} MR1080203
            %li
              %p
                V. G. Drinfelʹd,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quasi-Hopf algebras and Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov equations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shahn+Majid"}> Shahn Majid
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum double for quasi-Hopf algebras
                %strong 45
                (1998), no. 1, 1–9,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1631648"} MR2000b:16077
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1023/A:1007450123281"} doi
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9701002"} q-alg/9701002
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Schauenburg"}> Peter Schauenburg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hopf modules and the double of a quasi-Hopf algebra
                %strong 354
                (2002), 3349–3378
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/journals/tran/2002-354-08/S0002-9947-02-02980-X/S0002-9947-02-02980-X.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                M. Jimbo, H. Konno, S. Odake, J. Shiraishi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quasi-Hopf twistors for elliptic quantum groups
                %a{:href => "http://sci-hub.tw/10.1007/BF01238562"} doi
            %li
              %p
                Ivan Kobyzev, Ilya Shapiro,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A categorical approach to cyclic cohomology of quasi-Hopf algebras and Hopf algebroids
                = succeed ":1" do
                  %strong 27
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10485-018-9544-0"} doi
            %li
              %p
                L Frappat, D Issing, E Ragoucy,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    The quantum determinant of the elliptic algebra
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝒜
                            %mrow
                              %mi q
                              %mo ,
                              %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mover
                              %mi gl
                              %mo ^
                            %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}_{q, p}(\widehat{gl}_N)
                = succeed ":44," do
                  %strong A51
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1088/1751-8121/aae296"} doi
          %p See also Chapter 5, Sections 1, 11, and 12 of:
          %ul
            %li#EGNO16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Etingof"}> Pavel Etingof
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shlomo+Gelaki"} Shlomo Gelaki
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Nikshych"} Dmitri Nikshych
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Ostrik"} Victor Ostrik
              = succeed "." do
                %em Tensor categories
          %p A complete classification of quasi-Hopf algebras of dimension 6:
          %ul
            %li
              Daniel Bulacu, Matteo Misurati.
              %em
                Quasi-Hopf Algebras of Dimension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
